  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nRain/storm chances...Some CAMs are still picking up on a signal \nfor pop-up showers or storms (20 to 40% chance) north of Highway\n29 today due to weak instability. However, generating a shower or\nstorm will be difficult due to lack of forcing and smoke aloft. \nOverall, most areas should stay dry with upper ridging firmly in \nplace.\n\nBetter chances for a more widespread wetting rain will come on\nFriday as a potent shortwave spins up a surface low over the\nnorthern Plains. Current long-range solutions show more of a\nglancing blow to our southern tier of counties where higher PWATs\n(1.5 to 1.75\") are holed up closer to the low, though mid-range\nguidance shows pockets of heavier rainfall occurring with better\ninstability and skinny CAPE profiles Friday afternoon. Given the\nmore glancing blow, the threat for excessive rain this run appears\nto have fallen from previous forecasts along with the chance for\nrain, which currently stands at 40-60%. Convective activity \nshould remain fairly limited due to weak shear (< 20 knots). High \npressure then builds into the weekend, foretelling of a drier \nforecast into early next week. \n\nSmoke...GOES satellite imagery shows a plume of elevated wildfire\nsmoke in place over Wisconsin with high pressure in place across\nthe southern Great Lakes. Subsident airmass is expected to bring \nsome of this smoke to the surface at times, which should become\nmore widespread today. Air quality is forecast to fall into the \nmoderate (yellow) category during this time, with minor reductions\nin visibility possible. Worth noting is that overall air quality \nimpacts are not expected to be as drastic as earlier in the month.",
